Flow conditions on an open graph are not only a guarantee of the existence of a deterministic computation, but they also provide a recipe to obtain the deterministic pattern itself \cite{BKMP07:gflow}. In particular, the flow correction function and partial order prescribe a strongly deterministic pattern in the form of Eq.~\eqref{eq:pattern_xzcorr}, where the $\bm x$, $\bm z$ correction maps read as follows:
\begin{subequations}
    \begin{align}
    \text{Causal: }&\begin{cases}
        \bm x_{\text c}(i) = c(i),\\
        \bm z_{\text c}(i) = N_G(c(i)) \setminus \{i\},
    \end{cases} \label{eq:flow_to_corrections_causal}\\
    \text{Generalised: }&\begin{cases}
        \bm x_{\text g}(i) = g(i)\setminus \{i\},\\
        \bm z_{\text g}(i) = \operatorname{Odd}(g(i)) \setminus \{i\},
    \end{cases}\label{eq:flow_to_corrections_g}\\
    \text{Pauli: }&\begin{cases}
        \bm x_{\text p}(i) = p(i)\cap \{j | i \prec j\},\\
        \bm z_{\text p}(i) = \operatorname{Odd}(p(i)) \cap \{j | i \prec j\}, \label{eq:flow_to_corrections_pauli}
    \end{cases}
    \end{align}
\label{eq:flow_to_corrections}
\end{subequations}
where $g$ and $p$ are the gflow and Pauli flow correction functions (analogous to $c$ for the causal flow) and $\operatorname{Odd}(S) = \{v | N_G(v) \cap S = 1 \operatorname{mod} 2\}$ denotes the odd neighbourhood of a set $S$ in the open graph.

Graphix enables users to analyze the determinism of computations on open graphs using flows. The main types of flows discussed in the literature are represented by the classes \pythoninline{CausalFlow}, \pythoninline{GFlow} and \pythoninline{PauliFlow}. We implement the best-known complexity flow-finding algorithms so that all three types of flow objects can be extracted from a given \pythoninline{OpenGraph} instance, provided they exist (see Table \ref{tab:flows} and Section \ref{subsec:flows} for additional discussion).

